+
+    <h3>Why the corpora collapsed</h3>
+    <p>The two corpora that no single fixed schedule can cover are exactly the two whose policies used more than one schedule; all thirteen single-schedule-coverable corpora trained constant policies. The correspondence holds on <strong>15 of 15</strong> corpora. A Fisher exact figure of p ≈ 0.0095 is recorded in the decision note as prose; it appears in no measured artifact, and two qualifications belong with it: the fifteen corpora are 5 arms × 3 seeds over six shared contexts and one execution seed, so they are not fifteen independent draws, and the hypothesis was formed after seeing the table. Corpus complementarity is also necessary but not sufficient — one non-constant policy used three schedules and still selected the wrong one on the only context that discriminates. Non-constancy is a diagnostic, never the objective.</p>
+    <p>Two method components remain <strong>untested</strong> rather than supported. Complete-continuation teaching was common to all five arms, so nothing in this panel isolates it. Historical observation-gated replay is a measured null — identical to executed contrast in all eighteen cells, down to the selected schedule — and is reported as a tested component, not a contribution.</p>
